  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 150 PM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe upper high will begin nosing back east to over the Four Corners \narea Sunday before flattening across much of the desert SW and \nsouthern Great Basin Monday and beyond. Drier air moving in from the \nnorth will lower PWATS around 0.6 to 0.9 inches resulting in a much \nlower storm coverage. Isolated to widely scattered showers and \nstorms will be limited to the mountains and nearby highlands Sunday \nand Monday. With the 594 to 596 dam 500 mb upper high overhead along \nwith downslope southwest and west winds, the heat will reintensify \nacross central and eastern NM with upper 90s to mid 100s common \nacross lower elevations. Heat Advisories will likely be needed again \nacross the Northwest and West Central Plateau Sunday and will \npotentially need to be expanded to several central and eastern zones \ncome Monday. PWATS gradually increase to around 1 to 1.1 inches \nbeginning Tuesday helping to result in a slightly higher coverage of \nshowers and thunderstorms across the western and central high \nterrain and nearby lower elevations. Shower and thunderstorm \ncoverage appears to shift slightly west to areas along and west of \nthe central mountain chain late next week as the upper high becomes \nmore centered over Nevada and strengthens to ~ 597-599 dam at 500 \nmb. Temperatures will still be hotter than normal for early August \nwith highs across lower elevations topping out in the mid 90s to low \n100s.",
